The '''2022 United States Senate election in Kentucky''' was held on November 8, 2022, to elect a member of the [[United States Senate]] to represent [[Kentucky]]. Incumbent [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] [[Rand Paul]] won re-election to a third term, defeating [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] nominee [[Charles Booker (American politician)|Charles Booker]] with 61.8% of the vote.

Paul was first elected in [[2010 United States Senate election in Kentucky|2010]] with 55.7% of the vote, filling the seat of retiring [[Jim Bunning]], then re-elected in [[2016 United States Senate election in Kentucky|2016]] with 57.3% of the vote. Paul ran for a third term.<ref name="WBKO ABC">{{cite web|last=Dean|first=Kelly|date=Feb 25, 2021|title=Sen. Rand Paul discusses upcoming plans for re-election 2022|url=https://www.wbko.com/2021/02/25/sen-rand-paul-discusses-upcoming-plans-for-re-election-2022/|url-status=live|access-date=March 6, 2021|website=[[WBKO]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210227035322/https://www.wbko.com/2021/02/25/sen-rand-paul-discusses-upcoming-plans-for-re-election-2022/ |archive-date=February 27, 2021 }}</ref> Booker is a former state representative, and was a candidate in the Democratic primary for the U.S. Senate in [[2020 United States Senate election in Kentucky|2020]]. The election was called for Paul shortly after polls closed in the state.<ref>{{Cite web |date=November 9, 2022 |title=Rand Paul reelected for third term |url=https://www.wkyt.com/2022/11/09/rand-paul-re-elected-third-term/ |access-date=2022-11-09 |website=WKYT |language=en}}</ref>

This was the biggest landslide victory for a U.S. Senate race in Kentucky since [[Mitch McConnell]]'s win in [[2002 United States Senate election in Kentucky|2002]].

== Background ==
Although [[Rand Paul]] supported a constitutional amendment limiting senators to two terms, he said, "I'm not in favor of term limits for some and not others. So I'm not in favor of people self-imposing term limits. I'm a co-sponsor of the constitutional amendment, but I will run again in 2022."<ref name="WBKO ABC"/> Kentucky held its primary election on May 17.<ref>{{cite web|author=WLKY Digital Team |url=https://www.wlky.com/article/kentucky-primary-election-2022-voting-polls-hours/39944129 |title=Voter guide: Kentucky primary election 2022 |publisher=Wlky.com |date=2022-05-10 |accessdate=2022-05-27}}</ref>

