== General election ==
Although Paul had initially pledged to serve only one term,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Selleck |first=Stacey |date=2015-09-28 |title=Rand Paul Signs U.S. Term Limits Pledge - U.S. Term Limits |url=https://www.termlimits.com/rand-paul-signs-u-s-term-limits-pledge/,%20https://www.termlimits.com/rand-paul-signs-u-s-term-limits-pledge/ |access-date=2024-06-30 |language=en-US}} {{Dead link|date=September 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref> he later reversed this stance and ran for re-election.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Sen. Rand Paul officially files for re-election |url=https://spectrumnews1.com/ky/louisville/news/2022/01/04/rand-paul-runs-for-re-election |access-date=2024-06-30 |website=spectrumnews1.com |language=en}}</ref> In both [[2010 United States Senate election in Kentucky|2010]] and [[2016 United States Senate election in Kentucky|2016]], he faced tight races, even as these years were generally unfavorable for the Democratic Party and Kentucky's conservative partisan lean. Paul had gained a reputation as one of the most libertarian senators and often breaks with his party despite still holding conservative views on most issues.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Willis |first=Derek |date=2015-08-12 |title=Votes Against Party by Rand Paul (R-Ky.) |url=https://projects.propublica.org/represent/members/P000603/votes-against-party/116 |access-date=2024-06-30 |website=ProPublica |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Rand Paul on the Issues |url=https://www.ontheissues.org/senate/Rand_Paul.htm |access-date=2024-06-30 |website=www.ontheissues.org}}</ref>

Following a narrow primary defeat by [[Amy McGrath]] in the [[2020 United States Senate election in Kentucky|2020 Kentucky Senate Democratic primaries]], State Representative Charles Booker announced his intention to run again, this time against Paul.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sonka |first=Joe |title=Charles Booker makes it official, announces run for US Senate seat held by Rand Paul |url=https://www.courier-journal.com/story/news/politics/2021/07/01/charles-booker-announces-run-for-senate-against-rand-paul/7798435002/ |access-date=2024-06-30 |website=The Courier-Journal |language=en-US}}</ref> Booker positioned himself as a progressive populist, advocating for abortion rights, Universal Basic Income, Medicare for All, and a Green New Deal.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Charles Booker on the Issues |url=https://www.ontheissues.org/Senate/Charles_Booker.htm |access-date=2024-06-30 |website=www.ontheissues.org}}</ref> He aimed to resonate with traditional Democratic voters in the urban centers of Louisville and Lexington, while also reaching out to ancestral Democrats in Kentucky's Appalachian region.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Stand up for Kentucky Coal Miners |url=https://charlesbooker.org/issues/stand-up-for-kentucky-coal-miners/ |access-date=2024-06-30 |website=Charles Booker For U.S. Senate |language=en-US}}</ref>

An early February poll showed Paul leading by only a few points against a generic Democrat.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Watkins |first=Morgan |title=Mason-Dixon poll: Kentucky Sen. Rand Paul is in good shape for reelection next year |url=https://www.courier-journal.com/story/news/politics/2021/02/12/rand-paul-in-good-shape-for-reelection-poll-shows/6729324002/ |access-date=2024-06-30 |website=The Courier-Journal |language=en-US}}</ref> However, a later poll revealed Paul had a substantial lead over Booker.<ref>{{Cite web |title=2022 Kentucky Senate - Paul vs. Booker {{!}} RealClearPolling |url=https://www.realclearpolling.com/polls/senate/general/2022/kentucky/paul-vs-booker |access-date=2024-06-30 |website=www.realclearpolling.com |language=en}}</ref> In October, a debate was scheduled to include both Paul and Booker, but Paul did not respond to the invitation, resulting in Booker debating alone.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-09-30 |title=Rand Paul declines to debate Charles Booker on KET |url=https://www.lpm.org/news/2022-09-29/rand-paul-declines-to-debate-charles-booker-on-ket |access-date=2024-06-30 |website=Louisville Public Media |language=en}}</ref>

Paul went on to easily win re-election, improving his 2016 performance by approximately 9 percentage points. However, due to lower voter turnout, he underperformed compared to [[2020 United States presidential election in Kentucky|Trump's 2020 performance]] in the state by 2 points and received a slightly smaller percentage of the vote.

